{"id": "8501", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=8501", "title": "Crawfordville", "text": "Crawfordville is a city on the Forgotten Coast of Florida.\nGet in.\nThe main road that goes to Crawfordville is US Route 319, which connects it to Sopchoppy and Tallahassee. Shadeville Road, also called Route 61, goes from nearby Shadeville to Crawfordville.\nGet around.\nThe Crawfordville Highway is the main road, and goes from the north of the town to the south."}
{"id": "8502", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=8502", "title": "Crawley", "text": "Crawley is a bustling town of 119,000 people (2021) in the northern part of West Sussex, on the border with Surrey, in the South East of England. The biggest influence on the town is Gatwick Airport, which many people claiming it to being the 6th-busiest airport in the world.\nUnderstand.\nCrawley was a small hamlet until 1947 when it was designated a New Town by the government. Its population boomed in the 1950s and 1960s, due to this designation. From this, Crawley has become a multicultural town with a plethora of different ethnicities and religions, which can be seen in the food and the vibrant landscape of the town.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nCrawley marks the midpoint of the M23/A23, which links London in the north to Brighton on the south coast. The A217 leads north to Reigate, and the A264 heads east to East Grinstead and Tunbridge Wells, and west to Horsham.\nBy train.\nThere are three relevant train stops for Crawley:, and.\nWhile trains run directly and frequently from Gatwick and Three Bridges to Brighton, you will need to change in Three Bridges to Brighton when coming from Crawley Station. Fare and timetable information is available from Southeastern, tel. +44 345 748 4950.\nFrom Crawley station trains run north to London and Peterborough, and south to Horsham and Portsmouth. For railway services to Brighton and Haywards Heath, take a train heading north towards London Victoria or Bedford, and change at Three Bridges station (on the other side of Crawley) for trains to Brighton or Haywards Heath.\nRegular fast trains run direct from Gatwick to London Victoria, which are operated by a plethora of differrent railway companies.\nBy air.\n (), a major international airport and one of the world's busiest, is on the outskirts of Crawley. Gatwick Airport also has a railway station which can be used to take direct trains to Crawley.\nGet around.\nWithin the town centre, or to the station, county mall and the football stadium you can comfortably walk.\nBy bus.\nLocal buses run by Metrobus include routes: 10, 20, 100, 281, 291, 400. Fastway services are managed by Metrobus.\nFrom and to the airport take the number 10 Fastway service which runs almost always as a 24-hr service with the exception of Sundays and public holidays. There are also a number of alternative routes when travelling to the airport, such as routes 4 and 5.\nThere are also a multitude of other bus routes which can be used to reach the other suburbs, such as Pound Hill or Northgate.\nInformation about these routes and all the other routes can be found at the Metrobus Travel Shop, which is listed below:\nCrawley is also well renowned for its Hydroliners, a type of bus that runs on hydrogen fuel. These can be seen throughout the Fastway network, and mainly operate on routes 10 and 20.\nBy taxi.\nYou are able to find taxis at the main railway stations (Gatwick Airport, Three Bridges, Crawley) and by booking in advance via private hire companies in the area, which include:\nFares from the station to the airport are around \u00a314 and take 10 min (if there are no traffic jams). However, the Fastway bus 10 takes about 20 min and costs \u00a31.60.\nBy bicycle.\nCrawley is small enough to reach all ends cycling and in the town centre there are parking facilities. Cycle paths are marked and signed, but essentially the whole place is built for cars and especially the Gatwick Airport area is not meant for cyclists.\nEat.\nAlong the High Street are pubs and restaurants featuring typical English pubs and bars, Italian chains like Pizza Express, Si and Bella Italia, two or three curry houses, Mexican, kebab kiosks and more. There are also a lot of cheaper takeaways in Crawley where you can order a meal for around \u00a37 to \u00a315 if you're on a budget.\nConnect.\nAs of Feb 2023, Crawley and its approach roads have 4G from O2, and 5G from EE, Three and Vodafone.\nGo next.\nMap of places with Wikivoyage articles nearby\nOther places of interest in the Crawley area:"}
